 27200 Data Driven Bioengineering - Transcriptomics Workshop material Welcome Lecturer (theory): Lasse Ebdrup Pedersen (laeb@dtu.dk), Senior Researcher - DTU Bioengineering Responsibles: Juliana Assis, Senior Data Scientist - Biotechnology Research Institute for the Green Transition BRIGHT (jasge@dtu.dk)Sebastian Schulz, Senior Data Scientist - BRIGHT (sebschu@dtu.dk)Alberto Palleja Caro, Team Lead Data Science Platform - BRIGHT (apca@dtu.dk) Welcome to the Transcriptomics week of 27200 Data-driven Bioengineering! This session is Week 6 of the course (lecture: Thursday, 08 October 2026), part of the omics block. The theme of the week: transcript abundance as a dynamic - but incomplete - regulatory readout. We cover the theory behind RNA sequencing and the full practical workflow - from raw reads to biological interpretation: the RNA-seq workflow and count matrices, normalization, batch effects revisited, PCA, differential expression, and what transcriptomics reflects and misses. The material is designed for people with no or little practical experience in transcriptomic analysis using RNA sequencing (RNA-seq). Objectives Theory on Illumina short-read sequencing technology for transcriptome analysis Theory and practical sessions for RNA-seq data processing using the nf-core/rnaseq pipeline Quality control and exploratory analysis (normalization, PCA, sample correlation) Differential expression analysis with DESeq2 - design formulas, shrinkage, multiple testing Enrichment analysis (GSEA, plus ORA in the advanced bacterial track) - gaining biological insights from transcriptomic data Critically assessing what transcript abundance can and cannot tell you about biology Datasets 🧑‍🔬 Homo sapiens - airway smooth muscle cells treated with dexamethasone (paired donor design). This is the one we work on together. 🦠 Staphylococcus aureus - biofilm vs planktonic over time, strains USA-100 and USA-500. Advanced material, if you want to go further. Work through the human dataset: the three notebooks in order (01 quality control → 02 differential expression → 03 functional enrichment). Each one saves results that the next one reads, so the order matters. Depth beats coverage: it is better to interpret one dataset well than to rush. If you finish early, the S. aureus material takes the same ideas further, with a time course and two strains. You can also explore the 27200 Data Driven Bioengineering - Transcriptomics repository Draft agenda (4-hour block: lecture + guided exercises, in groups of 4) Time Session ☕ 8:00–8:15 Welcome and setup 🔬 8:15–9:00 Theory: Illumina sequencing, RNA-seq workflow &amp; the nf-core/rnaseq pipeline 📊 9:00–9:45 Theory: count matrices, normalization, PCA &amp; differential expression (+ quizzes) ⏸️ 9:45–10:00 Break 💻 10:00–10:55 Exercises: Quality Control &amp; Exploratory Data Analysis 🧬 10:55–11:50 Exercises: Differential Expression &amp; Functional Enrichment (GSEA) 🏁 11:50–12:00 Wrap-up: key insight + muddy point Material for the workshop is located at: dsp_transcriptomics_27200-Data-driven-bioengineering How to run the practicals. Use GitHub Codespaces - that is what we use in class, it needs nothing installed, and everyone gets an identical environment. The other two options below are for people who specifically want them, and are not supported during the session. Option 1: Codespaces (recommended) ⚠️ Before you start: change the Machine type to 4-core when you create the Codespace. When it has started, open 02_notebooks/ and begin with the human dataset. Option 2: Docker The Codespace runs from an image you can also start yourself. It already contains R, all the packages, Jupyter with the R kernel, and Nextflow. From the folder where you cloned the repository: Code docker run --rm -it -p 8888:8888 -v &quot;$PWD&quot;:/work -w /work \ ghcr.io/biosustain/dsp_transcriptomics_27200-data-driven-bioengineering:1.1.0 \ jupyter lab --ip=0.0.0.0 --port=8888 --no-browser --allow-root Open the http://127.0.0.1:8888/la