 Setup the Environment 📌 Remember: Load all libraries at the start of every session before running any analysis. Code library(tidyverse) library(reshape2) library(DESeq2) library(ggpubr) library(RColorBrewer) library(pheatmap) library(knitr) library(kableExtra) library(DT) Getting the Metadata The metadata file describes the experimental design: which sample belongs to which donor and treatment group. This information is essential for DESeq2 to model expression differences correctly. Code git_root &lt;- system(&quot;git rev-parse --show-toplevel&quot;, intern = TRUE) samples_info &lt;- read.table( file.path(git_root, &quot;data&quot;, &quot;data-02-Homo_sapiens&quot;, &quot;metadata&quot;, &quot;metadata.tsv&quot;), header = TRUE, sep = &quot;	&quot;, check.names = TRUE ) 💡 Tip: Use the interactive table below to verify your sample metadata before proceeding. Code DT::datatable( data = samples_info, rownames = FALSE, extensions = c(&#39;Buttons&#39;, &#39;Scroller&#39;), options = list( dom = &#39;Bfrtip&#39;, buttons = c(&#39;copy&#39;, &#39;csv&#39;), deferRender = TRUE, scrollX = TRUE, scrollY = 200, scroller = TRUE ), caption = &#39;Sample metadata - Human ASM (dexamethasone vs untreated)&#39; ) Loading Count Data The nf-core/rnaseq pipeline was run with STAR for alignment and Salmon for quantification. We load the SummarizedExperiment object produced by the pipeline, extract the raw count matrix, and assign gene symbols as row names. ⭐ Important: Salmon returns estimated counts, which are not integers. DESeq2 requires integer counts, so we round (not truncate) before building the object. This matches the tximport convention used by nf-core/differentialabundance. 💡 Tip: If you are unsure which assay name or rowData columns are available in your RDS, inspect them first with assayNames(count_x) and names(rowData(count_x)). Code count_x &lt;- readRDS( file.path(git_root, &quot;data&quot;, &quot;data-02-Homo_sapiens&quot;, &quot;hasapiens&quot;, &quot;star_salmon&qu