 Sanity Checks 📌 Remember: Always do a sanity check! Library sizes Before anything else, check how many reads ended up in each sample. DESeq2 needs raw, un-normalised integer counts; if you feed it TPM or FPKM the results are wrong. Code options(scipen = 999) barplot(colSums(count_genes), main = &quot;Library sizes (total counts per sample)&quot;, ylab = &quot;Total raw counts&quot;, xlab = NULL, col = &quot;steelblue&quot;, las = 2, names.arg = colnames(count_genes)) Samples differ in depth, which is exactly what the size factors further down correct for. What would worry you is one sample far below the rest: too few reads means too little information about that sample, whatever you do afterwards. Pre-filtering Low-count Genes Genes with very few counts across all samples carry no statistical power and inflate the multiple testing burden. We remove genes that do not have at least 10 counts in a minimum number of samples (equal to the size of the smallest group). The human genome has roughly 20,000 protein-coding genes plus many non-coding ones, and a large fraction are not expressed in ASM cells - expect a substantial number to be filtered out. Code smallestGroupSize &lt;- min(table(samples_info$condition)) cat(&quot;Smallest group size :&quot;, smallestGroupSize, &quot;
&quot;) ## Smallest group size : 4 Code cat(&quot;Filtering threshold : at least 10 counts in&quot;, smallestGroupSize, &quot;or more samples
&quot;) ## Filtering threshold : at least 10 counts in 4 or more samples Code keep &lt;- rowSums(counts(dds) &gt;= 10) &gt;= smallestGroupSize n_before &lt;- nrow(dds) dds &lt;- dds[keep, ] cat(&quot;Genes before filtering:&quot;, n_before, &quot;
&quot;) ## Genes before filtering: 78941 Code cat(&quot;Genes after filtering:&quot;, nrow(dds), &quot;
